# @@TITLE@@

@@ONE_LINE@@

## Why this exists

The naive way to answer this is to read files or dump AST JSON into the
conversation, both of which burn thousands of tokens. This skill pushes the work
into a small bundled script so the model reads only the result table: the script
runs `ast-grep`, computes the metric internally, and prints a small ranked table. You only ever see the table. Never pipe raw
`ast-grep --json` output into your own context to re-rank it by hand.

## Prerequisites

- `ast-grep` on PATH (`ast-grep --version`). Install: https://ast-grep.github.io
- Python 3 to run the bundled script.

## Usage

```bash
python "<skill_dir>/scripts/@@NAME@@.py" [PATH] [--top N] [--lang L] [--min N] [--include-tests]
```

`<skill_dir>` is the directory containing this SKILL.md. `PATH` defaults to the
current directory; languages are auto-detected unless `--lang` is given.

The output is already final-form, a `@@VALUE_COL@@ / @@NAME_COL@@ / LOCATION`
table sorted highest-first.

**Print the entire table to the user verbatim.** It IS the answer. Do NOT replace
it with a summary or describe it in prose, the user wants every row. You may add
ONE optional takeaway line after the table, but the full table comes first and in
full. Do not re-read the listed files unless the user then asks you to refactor one.

## Caveats

- The score is a refactor *hotspot* signal, not a verdict; `LOCATION` is
  authoritative.
- Only languages the engine supports are scored; pass `--lang` and sanity-check
  if something you expected shows nothing.
- `--min` defaults to 1. Raise it to focus on the worst offenders.
- Tests excluded by default (`*.spec.*`, `*.test.*`); add `--include-tests`.
  `node_modules`, `dist`, `build` and similar are always skipped.
